  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nBenign weather with warming conditions will continue this afternoon \nthrough tomorrow. This afternoon a surface ridge that was parked \nover the local area the past 24 hours is sliding east of the CWA and \nmodest WAA has developed in its wake. This will allow highs to top \nout in the mid to upper 80s today. Tonight a upper level trough will \ndig through the northern Plains into the Upper Midwest forcing an \nattendant surface front toward the CWA which will be located along a \nline from central Iowa to southeast Missouri to central Kansas by \ntomorrow morning. This front will very slowly progress south and \neast tomorrow the area during the day tomorrow. Continued modest WAA \nout ahead of the front will continue the warming trend for tomorrow \nwith highs in the upper 80s to mid 90s. A reinforcing upper level \nshortwave rounding the base of the trough over the northern Plains \nwill force the cold front into the northern CWA Tuesday night. This \nwill bring the next chance of thunderstorms to northern Missouri on \nTuesday night for areas north of the Missouri River (15%-30% from \nthe Missouri River to Highway 36 to 30-50% for areas from Highway 36 \nto the MO/IA border). Shower and storms and residual cloud cover may \nlinger through the morning on Wednesday however, current thinking is \nconditions will clear out for the late morning and afternoon \nparticularly along and south of the front. This will create a wide \ntemperature spread across the area on Wednesday with highs in the \nupper 70s across northern Missouri behind the front but will rise \ninto the mid 80s to lower 90s south of the front. This will create a \nconditional marginal severe threat Wednesday evening into Wednesday \nnight south of the front where MUCAPE values are expected to reach \n1500-2500J/Kg with effective shear of 45-55kts, damaging winds and \nlarge hail will be the main threats. Storms are expected to continue \nthrough the overnight before the front exits the area Thursday \nmorning. \n\nThursday and Friday the upper level flow will feature weak upper \nlevel troughing across the Great Lakes with amplified upper level \nridging over much of the western CONUS. This will leave the local \narea on the lee side of the ridge. Weak shortwave round the ridge \nThursday and Friday will continue storms chances each day (20-40%) \nwith greater chance across the southern CWA. Saturday the upper \nlevel ridge over the western CONUS begins to build eastward with the \nlocal area coming under its periphery will will allow for drier and \nwarmer conditions for the weekend. Highs Thursday and Friday will be \nin he 80s but will rise into the upper 80s to lower 90s by Saturday \nand into the low to mid 90s by Sunday.",
